<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="divisor-based-apportionment-methods">
    <title>Divisor-Based Apportionment Methods</title>

    <outcomes>
        <ol marker="1)">
            <li><p>Explain why and how a standard divisor is adjusted.</p></li>
            <li><p>Determine an apportionment using Jefferson's method.</p></li>
            <li><p>Determine an apportionment using Webster's method.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use geometric means to apply the Huntington-Hill rounding rule.</p></li>
            <li><p>Determine an apportionment using the Huntington-Hill method.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compare the allocations and biases of the three divisor-based methods.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain the tradeoff described by the Balinski-Young Impossibility Theorem.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </outcomes>

    <p>
        Jefferson's, Webster's, and Huntington-Hill methods are divisor-based methods. Each
        begins with the standard divisor and standard quotas. The quotas are rounded using a
        rule specific to the method. If the rounded allocations do not use the required number
        of items, the divisor is adjusted and the quotas are recalculated.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="modified-divisors">
        <title>Modified Divisors</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-modified-divisor">
            <title>Modified Divisor</title>
            <statement><p>

            </p></statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            A modified divisor, also called an adjusted divisor, replaces the standard divisor
            when the initial rounded allocations do not have the required total. Dividing by a
            smaller divisor produces larger quotas; dividing by a larger divisor produces
            smaller quotas.
        </p>

        <p>
            Therefore, if the rounded allocations total too few items, decrease the divisor.
            If they total too many items, increase the divisor. There is usually an interval of
            modified divisors that produces the desired allocation, so the modified divisor is
            not normally unique.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-adjust-divisor-direction">
            <title>Choosing the Direction of an Adjustment</title>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>A rounded allocation assigns 28 of 30 available seats. Should the divisor be increased or decreased?</p></li>
                <li><p>A rounded allocation assigns 42 of 40 available positions. Should the divisor be increased or decreased?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain why decreasing a divisor increases every recalculated quota.</p></li>
                <li><p>Why might several different modified divisors produce the same whole-number allocation?</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="jefferson-apportionment-method">
        <title>Jefferson's Method</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-jefferson-method">
            <title>Jefferson's Method</title>
            <statement><p>

            </p></statement>
        </definition>

        <p>To apply Jefferson's method:</p>
        <ol marker="1)">
            <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
            <li><p>Round every quota down to obtain an initial allocation.</p></li>
            <li><p>If the total is too small, decrease the divisor and recalculate the quotas.</p></li>
            <li><p>Continue rounding down and adjusting until the allocations have the required total.</p></li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Jefferson's method always rounds down after the quotas are calculated. Lowering
            the divisor increases the large quotas by greater absolute amounts, so Jefferson's
            method tends to favor larger groups. It is not guaranteed to satisfy the quota rule.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-jefferson-library-staff">
            <title>Apportioning Library Staff</title>
            <p>
                A library system apportions 25 staff positions among four branches serving
                populations of 825, 610, 405, and 160 patrons.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-jefferson-library-staff">
                <title>Branch Populations</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Branch</cell><cell>A</cell><cell>B</cell><cell>C</cell><cell>D</cell><cell>Total</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Population</cell><cell>825</cell><cell>610</cell><cell>405</cell><cell>160</cell><cell>2,000</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Round every standard quota down. How many positions are assigned?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain why the divisor must be decreased.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use 76 as a modified divisor and calculate the modified quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Round the modified quotas down and state the Jefferson apportionment.</p></li>
                <li><p>Verify that all 25 positions have been assigned.</p></li>
                <li><p>Does this apportionment satisfy the quota rule?</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-jefferson-test-divisors">
            <title>Testing Possible Modified Divisors</title>
            <p>
                A four-region system has populations 720, 510, 330, and 240 and must
                apportion 24 seats.
            </p>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Find the standard divisor and initial Jefferson allocation.</p></li>
                <li><p>Test divisors 74, 72, and 65.</p></li>
                <li><p>Which tested divisor produces the required total?</p></li>
                <li><p>If a tested divisor assigns too many seats, in which direction should the next trial move?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain why finding a modified divisor is a controlled search rather than an arbitrary guess.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="webster-apportionment-method">
        <title>Webster's Method</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-webster-method">
            <title>Webster's Method</title>
            <statement><p>

            </p></statement>
        </definition>

        <p>To apply Webster's method:</p>
        <ol marker="1)">
            <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
            <li><p>Round every quota to the nearest whole number.</p></li>
            <li><p>If the rounded total is too small, decrease the divisor; if it is too large, increase the divisor.</p></li>
            <li><p>Recalculate and round until the allocations have the required total.</p></li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Webster's method can require an adjustment in either direction. Ordinary rounding
            reduces the large-group advantage associated with Jefferson's method, although
            Webster's method is still not guaranteed to satisfy the quota rule.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-webster-outreach-coordinators">
            <title>Apportioning Outreach Coordinators</title>
            <p>
                Four service areas report 170, 215, 205, and 560 monthly requests. An agency
                apportions 23 outreach coordinators among them.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-webster-outreach-coordinators">
                <title>Monthly Requests by Service Area</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Area</cell><cell>A</cell><cell>B</cell><cell>C</cell><cell>D</cell><cell>Total</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Monthly requests</cell><cell>170</cell><cell>215</cell><cell>205</cell><cell>560</cell><cell>1,150</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Round the standard quotas to the nearest whole number. How many coordinators are assigned?</p></li>
                <li><p>Should the divisor be increased or decreased?</p></li>
                <li><p>Use 48.6 as a modified divisor and calculate the modified quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Round the modified quotas and state the Webster apportionment.</p></li>
                <li><p>Verify that the allocation uses all 23 coordinators.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="huntington-hill-apportionment-method">
        <title>Huntington-Hill Method</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-geometric-mean-apportionment">
            <title>Geometric Mean</title>
            <statement><p>

            </p></statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Let <m>n</m> be a quota's lower quota. The Huntington-Hill cutoff between
            <m>n</m> and <m>n+1</m> is their geometric mean:
            <me>\sqrt{n(n+1)}.</me>
            If the quota is greater than the geometric mean, round up. If it is less than the
            geometric mean, round down. These notes avoid the rare case in which a quota is
            exactly equal to the geometric mean.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-huntington-hill-method">
            <title>Huntington-Hill Method</title>
            <statement><p>

            </p></statement>
        </definition>

        <p>To apply the Huntington-Hill method:</p>
        <ol marker="1)">
            <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
            <li><p>For each quota, find its lower quota <m>n</m> and calculate <m>\sqrt{n(n+1)}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compare each quota with its geometric-mean cutoff and round accordingly.</p></li>
            <li><p>If the total is too small, decrease the divisor; if it is too large, increase the divisor.</p></li>
            <li><p>Continue until the rounded allocations have the required total.</p></li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Huntington-Hill rounding is based on relative, or percentage, differences rather
            than the fixed 0.5 cutoff used by Webster.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-huntington-hill-rounding">
            <title>Comparing Quotas with Geometric Means</title>
            <p>Apply the Huntington-Hill rounding rule to each quota.</p>
            <table xml:id="table-huntington-hill-rounding-practice">
                <title>Huntington-Hill Rounding Practice</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Quota</cell><cell>Lower quota</cell><cell>Geometric mean</cell><cell>Rounded value</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>2.460</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>4.410</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>6.490</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>9.520</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Complete the table.</p></li>
                <li><p>Which quotas are rounded differently by Huntington-Hill and ordinary rounding?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain why the Huntington-Hill cutoff is not always 0.5 above the lower quota.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-huntington-hill-field-technicians">
            <title>Apportioning Field Technicians</title>
            <p>
                Four environmental regions report 650, 915, 600, and 750 monitoring visits.
                An agency apportions 29 field technicians among them.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-huntington-hill-field-technicians">
                <title>Monitoring Visits by Region</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Region</cell><cell>A</cell><cell>B</cell><cell>C</cell><cell>D</cell><cell>Total</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Monitoring visits</cell><cell>650</cell><cell>915</cell><cell>600</cell><cell>750</cell><cell>2,915</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the lower quota and geometric mean for each region.</p></li>
                <li><p>Apply Huntington-Hill rounding. How many technicians are initially assigned?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain why the divisor must be decreased.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use 100.25 as a modified divisor and recalculate the quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Apply the geometric-mean rule and state the final apportionment.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="compare-divisor-apportionment-methods">
        <title>Comparing the Divisor-Based Methods</title>

        <example xml:id="ex-compare-three-divisor-methods">
            <title>Three Methods, Three Allocations</title>
            <p>
                A country apportions 32 legislative seats among five regions with the
                populations shown below.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-three-divisor-methods-comparison">
                <title>Regional Populations</title>
                <tabular row-headers="yes" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Region</cell><cell>A</cell><cell>B</cell><cell>C</cell><cell>D</cell><cell>E</cell><cell>Total</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Population</cell><cell>870</cell><cell>495</cell><cell>310</cell><cell>165</cell><cell>160</cell><cell>2,000</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Find the standard divisor and standard quotas.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use Jefferson's method with modified divisor 58.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use Webster's method with modified divisor 64.1.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use the Huntington-Hill method with modified divisor 64.5.</p></li>
                <li><p>Verify that each method assigns exactly 32 seats.</p></li>
                <li><p>Which method gives the largest region the most seats?</p></li>
                <li><p>Which method gives the smallest region the most seats?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain how the three rounding rules produce the differences.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-divisor-methods-quota-rule">
            <title>Checking the Quota Rule</title>
            <p>Use the standard quotas and allocations from the preceding example.</p>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Find the lower and upper quota for each region.</p></li>
                <li><p>Does the Jefferson allocation satisfy the quota rule?</p></li>
                <li><p>Does the Webster allocation satisfy the quota rule?</p></li>
                <li><p>Does the Huntington-Hill allocation satisfy the quota rule?</p></li>
                <li><p>Identify any region receiving less than its lower quota or more than its upper quota.</p></li>
                <li><p>Why does one successful example not prove that a method always follows the quota rule?</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-identify-divisor-method">
            <title>Identifying the Divisor Method</title>
            <p>Identify the method described in each statement.</p>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Every recalculated quota is rounded down.</p></li>
                <li><p>Every recalculated quota is rounded to the nearest whole number.</p></li>
                <li><p>A quota is compared with <m>\sqrt{n(n+1)}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>The method tends to favor larger groups most strongly.</p></li>
                <li><p>The rounding decision is based on relative differences between neighboring whole numbers.</p></li>
                <li><p>The divisor may need to be increased or decreased depending on the initial total.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="balinski-young-theorem">
        <title>A Limitation of Apportionment Methods</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-balinski-young-impossibility-theorem">
            <title>Balinski-Young Impossibility Theorem</title>
            <statement><p>

            </p></statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            The Balinski-Young Impossibility Theorem states that an apportionment method that
            is guaranteed to satisfy the quota rule must be vulnerable to apportionment
            paradoxes such as the Alabama, population, or new states paradox. Avoiding those
            paradoxes requires giving up the guarantee that every allocation will satisfy the
            quota rule.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-apportionment-tradeoff">
            <title>Explaining the Tradeoff</title>
            <ol marker="1)">
                <li><p>Which two methods from the previous section always satisfy the quota rule?</p></li>
                <li><p>What kinds of paradoxes can affect those methods?</p></li>
                <li><p>Are Jefferson, Webster, and Huntington-Hill guaranteed to satisfy the quota rule?</p></li>
                <li><p>Why does the theorem prevent us from selecting a perfect method using only these properties?</p></li>
                <li><p>Explain why the choice of an apportionment method involves a fairness judgment.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <conclusion>
        <p>
            The mathematical ideas in this section are based on the apportionment chapter of
            Mike Kenyon and David Lippman's <em>Math in Society</em>, licensed under Creative
            Commons BY-SA. The contexts, numerical examples, and questions in these notes
            are new.
        </p>
    </conclusion>
</section>
